---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Massachusetts school spending explorer - Tim Shores"
  description="Per-pupil spending, enrollment, Chapter 70 aid, and revenue for every Massachusetts school district, and the general fund budgets of all 351 cities and towns, FY2009 to FY2025"
>
  <section id="explorer" class="explorer is-loading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Massachusetts · FY2009–FY<span data-latest>2025</span></p>
    <h1>School spending explorer</h1>
    <details class="intro" id="intro" data-remember="intro">
    <summary>About this explorer</summary>
    <p class="lede">
      Why do our <span class="swap" data-districts="schools" data-towns="municipal services">schools</span> cost so
      much? Or are we not spending enough tax dollars on our
      <span class="swap" data-districts="schools" data-towns="towns">schools</span>? These are difficult questions to
      answer because it depends on your basis of comparison. Use this Explorer to discover the cost of your
      <span class="swap" data-districts="school" data-towns="town">school</span>.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      This explorer continues the work of my report on
      <a href="/artifacts/western-ma-ppe-report-fy2025.pdf">per-pupil expenditure in Western Massachusetts</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="lede"><em>You'll probably have a better experience exploring on a desktop than a mobile browser. I'll work on making that better.</em></p>
    </details>
    <script is:inline>
      // Open unless this browser remembers it closed; decided here, before the page paints, so it
      // never flashes. The section is the element just before this script (explorer.ts remembers changes).
      (function () {
        var d = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;
        var closed = false;
        try {
          closed = localStorage.getItem("mass-school-spending:remember") === "yes" &&
            localStorage.getItem("mass-school-spending:" + d.dataset.remember) === "closed";
        } catch (e) {}
        if (!closed) d.open = true;
      })();
    </script>

    <div class="view-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Explorer view">
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-districts" data-view="districts" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="true">School districts</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-towns" data-view="towns" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Towns</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-commonwealth" data-view="commonwealth" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Commonwealth</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-cap" data-view="cap" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">The 82.5% cap</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-data" data-view="data" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Data</button>
    </div>

    <div id="view-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-districts">
    <form class="controls" onsubmit="return false" data-view-only="districts towns">
      <div class="control" data-view-only="districts">
        <span id="pick-district-label">District</span>
        <select id="pick-district" aria-labelledby="pick-district-label"></select>
      </div>
      <label class="control" data-view-only="districts">
        <span>Compare with</span>
        <select id="pick-compare">
          <option value="none">No comparison</option>
          <option value="cohort">Its cohort in its region</option>
          <option value="cohort-state">Its cohort statewide</option>
          <option value="region">Its region</option>
          <option value="state">Statewide</option>
          <option value="gateway">Gateway Cities</option>
          <option value="other">Another district or average</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <div class="control" id="vs-control" hidden>
        <span id="pick-vs-label">Comparison district</span>
        <select id="pick-vs" aria-labelledby="pick-vs-label"></select>
      </div>
      <div class="control" data-view-only="towns" hidden>
        <span id="pick-town-label">Town</span>
        <select id="pick-town" aria-labelledby="pick-town-label"></select>
      </div>
      <label class="control" data-view-only="towns" hidden>
        <span>Compare with</span>
        <select id="pick-town-compare">
          <option value="none">No comparison</option>
          <option value="peer">Its peer group in its region</option>
          <option value="peer-state">Its peer group statewide</option>
          <option value="region">Its region</option>
          <option value="state">Statewide</option>
          <option value="gateway">Gateway Cities</option>
          <option value="other">Another town or average</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <div class="control" id="town-vs-control" hidden>
        <span id="pick-town-vs-label">Comparison town or average</span>
        <select id="pick-town-vs" aria-labelledby="pick-town-vs-label"></select>
      </div>
      <fieldset class="control toggle">
        <legend>Dollars</legend>
        <div class="toggle-options">
          <label><input type="radio" name="dollars" value="nominal" checked /><span>Nominal</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="dollars" value="real" /><span>FY<span data-latest>2025</span> dollars</span></label>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
      <div class="control years-control">
        <span id="years-label">Years</span>
        <div class="years-slider" role="group" aria-labelledby="years-label">
          <div class="years-track">
            <div class="years-fill"></div>
            <input type="range" id="year-from" min="0" max="16" step="1" value="0" aria-label="First year shown" />
            <input type="range" id="year-to" min="0" max="16" step="1" value="16" aria-label="Last year shown" />
          </div>
          <output id="years-out">FY2009–FY2025</output>
        </div>
        <div class="years-ends" aria-hidden="true"><span>FY<span data-start>2009</span></span><span>FY<span data-latest>2025</span></span></div>
      </div>
      <fieldset class="control toggle" data-view-only="towns" hidden>
        <legend>Per</legend>
        <div class="toggle-options">
          <label><input type="radio" name="per" value="resident" checked /><span>Resident</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="per" value="household" /><span>Household</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="per" value="parcel" /><span>Taxable property</span></label>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
      <p class="controls-note" id="dollars-note"></p>
      <p class="controls-note" id="per-note" data-view-only="towns" hidden></p>
      <div class="relations" id="relations" hidden></div>
    </form>

    <header class="entity" data-view-only="districts towns">
      <h2 id="entity-name">Loading…</h2>
      <p id="entity-meta"></p>
    </header>

    <div id="tiles" class="tiles" data-view-only="districts towns"></div>

    <section id="cw-view" class="cw-view" data-view-only="commonwealth" hidden>
      <div id="cw-map"></div>
      <h3 class="cw-plots-title">Districts side by side</h3>
      <p class="cw-intro">
        Every Massachusetts school district side by side, two measures at a time. These plots are the pairs that
        line up most closely with the fewest cautions. Open one to choose other measures, the year, and which
        districts stand out, and to see which factors line up most closely with spending.
      </p>
      <div id="cw-root"><p class="tab-loading">Loading the plots…</p></div>
    </section>

    <section id="cap-view" class="cap-view" data-view-only="cap" hidden>
      <h2 class="cap-title">The 82.5% cap — <i>page very much still under development</i></h2>
      <details class="intro" id="cap-intro" data-remember="cap-intro">
      <summary>About this tab</summary>
      <p class="cap-lede">
        Massachusetts asks every town to pay a share of its wealth toward its schools, and the state pays the rest of
        what the Chapter 70 formula says those schools need. But no town is asked for more than 82.5% of that amount,
        its foundation budget. The towns with the most property and income for the size of their schools hit that cap
        and are asked for less. The difference doesn't disappear: the formula raises the share asked of every town
        below the cap to make it up.
      </p>
      <p class="cap-lede">
        <a href="https://masspromise2invest.org/">MASS Promise to Invest</a>, a statewide campaign for school funding,
        argues that the cap distributes costs unfairly. In the campaign's words, “To fund the reduced rate as a result
        of the cap for the wealthy districts, the lowest income districts essentially pay an increased amount.”
        Montague resident Michael Naughton worked out every town's FY2026 target with and without the cap from DESE's
        formula spreadsheet, and the campaign posted
        <a href="https://masspromise2invest.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/fy26-chapter-70-cap-comparisons.xlsx">his workbook</a>
        on <a href="https://masspromise2invest.org/information-2/">its information page</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="cap-lede">
        I like this work, and I'm curious about it. The arithmetic holds up in DESE's own spreadsheets, and the size of
        it surprised me. This tab is where I'm building tools to see who pays more because of the cap, who pays less,
        and by how much.
      </p>
      </details>
      <script is:inline>
        // Open unless this browser remembers it closed; decided here, before the page paints, so it
        // never flashes. The section is the element just before this script (explorer.ts remembers changes).
        (function () {
          var d = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;
          var closed = false;
          try {
            closed = localStorage.getItem("mass-school-spending:remember") === "yes" &&
              localStorage.getItem("mass-school-spending:" + d.dataset.remember) === "closed";
          } catch (e) {}
          if (!closed) d.open = true;
        })();
      </script>
      <div id="cap-root"><p class="tab-loading">Loading the Chapter 70 figures…</p></div>
      <section class="cap-section cap-questions">
        <h3>Questions I'm still working through</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Targets aren't bills.</strong> When a town's required contribution is above its target, it drops to
            the target. When it's below, it grows by the town's municipal revenue growth factor, plus 1 or 2 percent
            more if it's far behind. Would lifting the cap cut many towns' requirements at once and raise the wealthiest
            towns' over years? What happens in between?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where would the money go?</strong> Chapter 70 aid starts each year from the year before. A district
            whose aid and required contribution fall short of its foundation budget gets foundation aid to close the
            gap; the rest get a minimum increase, $160 per pupil in FY2027. How much would a higher target lower a
            wealthy town's aid, and what would lower targets elsewhere cost the state?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Who counts as lower income?</strong> The formula counts the income on residents' state tax returns.
            Amherst, where students count as residents, has one of the lowest incomes per resident in the state, yet it
            is at the cap in both years: with the cap's percentages, its property and income yield more than 82.5% of
            its foundation budget. How many towns would a different measure of income move?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Regional districts.</strong> A town's contribution is divided among its school districts by each
            one's share of the foundation budget for the town's pupils, so the cap reaches regional districts through
            their member towns. How does it add up district by district?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <p class="cap-method">
        <strong>Sources and method.</strong> Each town's figures come from DESE's complete formula spreadsheets for
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2026/chapter-2026.xlsm">FY2026</a> and
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2027/chapter-2027.xlsm">FY2027</a>, the Townwide
        Contributions sheet (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-chapter70-targets.csv" download>CSV</a>), and
        the statewide percentages and totals from DESE's summaries of the formula for
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2026/chapter-2026-whitepaper.docx">FY2026</a> and
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2027/chapter-2027-whitepaper.docx">FY2027</a>. For
        another cap, or none, I keep the formula's rule that targets add up to 59% of all foundation budgets and scale
        both percentages by the one factor that makes them add up; each town's target is then the smaller of its scaled
        yield and the new cap. For FY2026 this matches Naughton's targets with a 100% cap to the dollar, and with no cap
        to within a hundredth of a percent. Per-resident figures use the Census Bureau's July 2024 population
        estimates; income per resident divides the formula's income by the estimate for July of that year.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div id="panels" class="panels" data-view-only="districts">
      <figure id="fig-ppe" class="panel">
        <h3>Spending per pupil by category</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="ppe-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-ppe" aria-label="Spending categories. Select one or more to show only those."></ul>
        <svg id="svg-ppe" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Stacked columns of spending per pupil by category for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-ppe"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="ppe" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-enroll" class="panel">
        <h3>Enrollment</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="enroll-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-enroll"></ul>
        <svg id="svg-enroll" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Lines of in-district, foundation, and out-of-district enrollment for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-enroll"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="enroll" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-nss" class="panel">
        <h3>Chapter 70 aid and net school spending</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="nss-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-nss" aria-label="Net school spending components. Select one or more to show only those."></ul>
        <svg id="svg-nss" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Stacked columns of Chapter 70 aid, required local contribution, and spending above the requirement per foundation pupil for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-nss"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="nss" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-rev" class="panel">
        <h3>Revenue per pupil by source</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="rev-sub"></p>
        <p class="panel-none" id="rev-none" hidden></p>
        <div id="rev-body">
          <ul class="legend" id="legend-rev" aria-label="Revenue sources. Select one or more to show only those."></ul>
          <svg id="svg-rev" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Stacked columns of revenue per pupil by source for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
          <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
          <details class="table-view compare-view">
            <summary>Comparison table</summary>
            <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-rev"></div>
          </details>
          <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="rev" aria-haspopup="dialog">
            <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
          </button>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div id="town-panels" class="panels" data-view-only="towns" hidden>
      <figure id="fig-tspend" class="panel">
        <h3>General fund spending per <span data-per>resident</span> by function</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="tspend-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-tspend" aria-label="Spending functions. Select one or more to show only those."></ul>
        <svg id="svg-tspend" tabindex="0" role="img" data-aria-per="Stacked columns of general fund spending per {per} by function for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value." aria-label="Stacked columns of general fund spending per resident by function for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-tspend"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="tspend" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-trev" class="panel">
        <h3>General fund revenue per <span data-per>resident</span> by source</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="trev-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-trev" aria-label="Revenue sources. Select one or more to show only those."></ul>
        <svg id="svg-trev" tabindex="0" role="img" data-aria-per="Stacked columns of general fund revenue per {per} by source for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value." aria-label="Stacked columns of general fund revenue per resident by source for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-trev"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="trev" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-tlevy" class="panel">
        <h3>Property tax levy and its limit per <span data-per>resident</span></h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="tlevy-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-tlevy" aria-label="Levy and unused capacity. Select one to show only it."></ul>
        <svg id="svg-tlevy" tabindex="0" role="img" data-aria-per="Stacked columns of the property tax levy and unused levy capacity per {per} for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value." aria-label="Stacked columns of the property tax levy and unused levy capacity per resident for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-tlevy"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="tlevy" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-tbill" class="panel">
        <h3>Average single-family tax bill</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="tbill-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-tbill"></ul>
        <svg id="svg-tbill" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Line of the average single-family tax bill for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="tbill" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-tval" class="panel">
        <h3>Assessed value per <span data-per>resident</span> by property type</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="tval-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-tval" aria-label="Property types. Select one or more to show only those."></ul>
        <svg id="svg-tval" tabindex="0" role="img" data-aria-per="Stacked columns of assessed property value per {per} by property type for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value." aria-label="Stacked columns of assessed property value per resident by property type for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-tval"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="tval" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-tpar" class="panel">
        <h3>Parcels by property type</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="tpar-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-tpar" aria-label="Property types. Select one or more to show only those."></ul>
        <svg id="svg-tpar" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Stacked columns of parcels by property type for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <details class="table-view compare-view">
          <summary>Comparison table</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll" id="cmp-tpar"></div>
        </details>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="tpar" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-teqv" class="panel">
        <h3>Equalized valuation per <span data-per>resident</span></h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="teqv-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-teqv"></ul>
        <svg id="svg-teqv" tabindex="0" role="img" data-aria-per="Line of equalized valuation per {per}, every other fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value." aria-label="Line of equalized valuation per resident, every other fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="teqv" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>

      <figure id="fig-tpop" class="panel">
        <h3>Population</h3>
        <p class="panel-sub" id="tpop-sub"></p>
        <ul class="legend" id="legend-tpop"></ul>
        <svg id="svg-tpop" tabindex="0" role="img" aria-label="Line of population for each fiscal year. Use the arrow keys to read each year; the data table below has every value."></svg>
        <div class="tooltip" role="status" hidden></div>
        <button type="button" class="table-open" data-table="tpop" aria-haspopup="dialog">
          <span class="table-open-icon" aria-hidden="true">⤢</span> Full data table
        </button>
      </figure>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div id="remember-prompt" class="remember-prompt" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="remember-title" hidden>
      <p id="remember-title"><strong>Remember your choices?</strong></p>
      <p>
        This page can reopen to the districts, towns, and settings you choose. They stay in this browser and are
        never sent anywhere.
      </p>
      <div class="remember-actions">
        <button type="button" id="remember-yes">Remember</button>
        <button type="button" id="remember-no">No thanks</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <dialog id="table-dialog" class="table-dialog" aria-labelledby="table-dialog-title">
      <div class="dialog-head">
        <h3 id="table-dialog-title"></h3>
        <button type="button" class="dialog-close" aria-label="Close the table">×</button>
      </div>
      <div class="dialog-body table-scroll" id="table-dialog-body"></div>
    </dialog>

    <section class="notes" data-view-only="data" hidden>
      <h2>About the data</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Spending per pupil</strong> is the Massachusetts Department of Elementary and Secondary Education's
        (DESE) <a href="https://educationtocareer.data.mass.gov/d/er3w-dyti">per-pupil expenditure</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/districts-spending-per-pupil.csv" download>CSV</a>) from each district's end-of-year report, divided by in-district FTE and grouped
        into the report's nine categories; its Other combines DESE's professional development and instructional
        materials, equipment, and technology. It leaves out tuition for pupils educated elsewhere, because, as DESE puts
        it, those costs "vary greatly depending on the reason for going out of district."
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Enrollment</strong> comes in three measures. In-district FTE is full-year enrollment of students who
        live in the district or are choiced or tuitioned into it. Foundation enrollment counts the residents the
        district is financially responsible for on October 1 of the prior year. Out-of-district FTE counts the
        district's pupils educated elsewhere. In-district and out-of-district FTE come from
        <a href="https://educationtocareer.data.mass.gov/d/er3w-dyti">DESE's expenditure data</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/districts-spending-per-pupil.csv" download>CSV</a>), foundation enrollment from its
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/default.html">Chapter 70 district profiles</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/districts-chapter70.csv" download>CSV</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Chapter 70 aid and net school spending</strong> are per foundation pupil, from DESE's
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/default.html">Chapter 70 district profiles</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/districts-chapter70.csv" download>CSV</a>). The state sets a required
        level of net school spending for each district each year. Chapter 70 aid pays part of it, and the town, or a
        regional district's member towns, pays the required local contribution. Anything above that is a local choice. Because spending per pupil and
        net school spending use different denominators, the two charts are not the same measure: they are a picture
        of apples in the morning compared with a picture of apples in the evening. Both dollar charts share one scale.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Cohorts</strong> group traditional districts by FY<span data-latest>2025</span> in-district FTE, using
        the boundaries <a href="/artifacts/western-ma-ppe-report-fy2025.pdf">the report</a> set from FY2024 Western MA
        enrollment: Tiny up to 200 FTE, Small 201–800, Medium 801–1,600, Large 1,601–10,000, and Very large above
        that. Vocational districts and charter schools are groups of their own. Regions are the state's six EOHHS
        regions. As the report left Springfield out of its Western MA average, region and statewide averages leave out
        the ten very large districts, which have an average of their own. A region with fewer than three districts in a
        cohort has no regional average for it, so "its cohort in its region" uses the statewide cohort there. Spending
        averages are weighted by in-district FTE, Chapter 70 averages by foundation enrollment, and enrollment
        averages are per district. <a href="https://www.mass.gov/doc/gatewaycitiesdocx/download">Gateway Cities</a>
        are the 26 mid-size cities the Commonwealth lists, whose median household income and college attainment fall
        below the state's (the list dates from 2014). Their districts, and the cities on the Towns tab, have averages
        of their own, alongside their size cohorts and peer groups. Two virtual schools, the state's institutional schools, and the Massachusetts
        Academy of Math and Science are not included.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Revenue by source</strong> comes from the Census Bureau's
        <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/school-finances.html">Annual Survey of School System Finances</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/districts-revenue.csv" download>CSV</a>, in dollars, each column naming the Census items it adds up),
        divided by in-district FTE like spending. It covers regional districts only: for many town-run
        districts, Census's local revenue is only part of what the town spends on its schools (for Amherst in FY2024,
        $18.4 million of revenue against $37.6 million of spending). Payments from towns include a regional
        district's member-town assessments; payments from other school districts include tuition for pupils they
        send. Revenue per pupil usually runs about a third above spending per pupil, because it also pays for what
        the spending measure leaves out, such as tuition for pupils educated elsewhere, school buildings, and the
        teachers' pensions the state pays into the teachers' retirement system on districts' behalf. School building
        aid can arrive in one large payment. In FY2009 the state paid $412 million of Chapter 70 aid with federal
        stimulus money, so Census counts about a tenth of that year's Chapter 70 aid as federal. Census publishes
        each year's figures more than a year after it ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Town budgets</strong> come from each town's <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=ScheduleA.GenFund_MAIN">Schedule A</a>
        (CSVs: <a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-spending.csv" download>spending</a>, <a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-revenue.csv" download>revenue</a>, each with population), the year-end report of general fund revenue and
        spending that town accountants file with the state Division of Local Services. Enterprise, capital, and other
        funds are not included. Each town's school districts and FY2025 required local contributions come from DESE's
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2025/chapter-2025.html">Chapter 70 tables</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-school-districts.csv" download>CSV</a>), and each town takes the
        region of its own school district, or of its regional district.
        Peer groups put each town with others its size, by the July 2024 population estimate: under 1,000 residents
        (26 towns), 1,000–2,499 (42), 2,500–9,999 (98), 10,000–24,999 (103), 25,000–49,999 (55), and 50,000 or more
        (17). The ten cities whose school districts are very large, from Boston to Springfield, stand apart from the
        town averages, as their districts do from the district averages. Averages pool their towns per resident. Dollars are per
        resident, using the Census Bureau's <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest.html">population estimate</a> for July 1 at the start of each fiscal year. They can also be shown per household, using the Census Bureau's
        <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs">American Community Survey</a> five-year estimates
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-households.csv" download>CSV</a>), or per taxable property, using the
        parcels of taxable real estate on each town's LA-4. For FY2021
        the April 1, 2020 estimate stands in, because the July 2020 estimate drops by more than a quarter in Amherst
        and recovers a year later.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Property</strong> comes from the state Division of Local Services. Parcels and assessed values are
        each board of assessors' yearly <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=PropertyTaxInformation.LA4.Parcel_counts_vals">LA-4 report</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-property.csv" download>CSV</a>), as of January 1 before each fiscal
        year, grouped by the state's property use codes. Multifamily is two- and three-family homes and apartment
        buildings (codes 104, 105, 111–125). Other residential is mobile homes, lots with more than one house, and
        vacant or accessory residential land (103, 109, 130–132, 106). Mixed use is property with more than one use,
        such as a store with apartments above it (012–043). Farmland is land taxed under the Chapter 61A agricultural
        program; forest, recreation, and open space is land under Chapters 61 and 61B and class 2 open space. Personal
        property, such as business equipment and utility lines, is assessed but has no parcel. Exempt property, such
        as schools, churches, and public land, is left out. The
        <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=AverageSingleTaxBill.SingleFamTaxBill_wRange">average single-family tax bill</a>
        is the state's: single-family assessed value divided by single-family parcels, times the residential tax rate,
        set beside the Department of Revenue's income per capita. Amherst's FY2025 bill is 41% of its income per
        capita, the highest share in the state; income per capita counts every resident, students included.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The property tax levy</strong> is what a town raises from property taxes, and
        <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=Prop2.5.ExcessLevyCapandOverride_10_pres">Proposition 2½</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-levy.csv" download>CSV</a>) limits it. Each year a town's levy limit
        grows by 2.5% plus the tax on new construction, and voters can raise it with an override or exclude a debt
        from it; the charts' limit includes those exclusions. The limit itself can't pass the levy ceiling, 2.5% of the
        town's assessed value, though excluded debt can. Unused levy capacity is the gap between the limit and the levy a town sets: money it could
        raise without asking voters, and chooses not to. It can be read as tax relief, as careful budgeting, or as
        austerity. The tax rate here is the single-family bill over the home's value, which is the residential rate
        except where a town gives a residential exemption.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Equalized valuation (EQV)</strong> is the state's estimate of the full and fair cash value of all
        taxable property in each city and town on January 1, set every two years by the Commissioner of Revenue
        (<a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=PropertyTaxInformation.EQV.EQV">EQVs since 1970</a>;
        <a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-eqv.csv" download>CSV</a>). Assessors value property at different
        distances from market prices, so the state's Bureau of Local Assessment compares each town's assessments
        with its recent sales, class by class, and divides each class's assessed value by that ratio: homes assessed
        at 95% of their sale prices have their value divided by 0.95. It then adds an estimate of new construction
        (<a href="https://www.mass.gov/info-details/equalized-valuations-eqv">DLS</a>;
        <a href="https://www.mass.gov/doc/eqv-leveling-the-playing-field/download">“EQV: Leveling the Playing Field”</a>).
        Chapter 70 uses EQV to measure each town's property wealth when it sets what the town should contribute. In
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2025/chapter-2025-whitepaper.docx">FY2025</a>, a
        town's target local contribution was 0.3906% of its January 2022 EQV plus 1.4312% of its residents' 2021
        income. The state chose the two percentages so that, statewide, property and income would each supply half
        of a total equal to 59% of all foundation budgets. No town's target could exceed 82.5% of its foundation
        budget, and 182 towns were at that cap. Each year a town's required contribution moves from the year before
        toward its target, and Chapter 70 aid pays at least the rest of the foundation budget.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Town and school budgets overlap without lining up.</strong> Education includes the town's own school
        department and what it pays regional and vocational school districts, so it does not match any one district's
        spending, and health insurance and pensions for a town's own school employees usually sit in fixed costs.
        State aid includes Chapter 70 aid for a town's own school district; aid to a regional district goes to the
        district. Intergovernmental assessments are the state and county charges on each town's Cherry Sheet, such as
        charter school and school choice tuition. Required local contributions are what the
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2025/chapter-2025.html">Chapter 70 formula</a> sets
        for each town and district in FY2025.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Revenue groups</strong> combine <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=ScheduleA.GenFund_MAIN">Schedule A</a>'s eleven sources into six: charges, fees, and fines are
        service charges, licenses and permits, fines and forfeitures, and special assessments; other revenue is
        miscellaneous revenue and revenue from other governments; transfers and other financing are transfers and
        other financing sources. Figures are as each town filed them, and a few look like one-time entries: Lee's
        FY2024 revenue includes $27.3 million of miscellaneous revenue, more than half its total.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>FY<span data-latest>2025</span> dollars</strong> adjust for inflation with the Bureau of Labor
        Statistics' <a href="https://data.bls.gov/timeseries/CUUR0100SA0">consumer price index for the Northeast</a>
        (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/cpi.csv" download>CSV</a>; CPI-U, series CUUR0100SA0), averaged over each July–June
        fiscal year. The report's
        <a href="/artifacts/western-ma-ppe-inflation-addendum-fy2025.pdf">inflation addendum</a> restates its growth
        rates against this index and against the federal price index for state and local government purchases.
      </p>
      <p>
        The CSVs hold the data for these districts and towns as each agency published it, except that the revenue
        file adds Census items up into the chart's sources, and every row links to its source. Built by Tim Shores with Claude Code. The code that prepares the data is at
        <a href="https://github.com/timshores/schools">github.com/timshores/schools</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- On every tab: whether this browser keeps the reader's choices -->
